post-TTL surface sweep (F61) -- r132, 2026-08-12
=================================================

fable's r132 item 1.  Its own retraction of the round before is the reason this exists: a
cold fetch of a MUTABLE path served a render from one push earlier, with a defect that had
already been fixed, and the draft report nearly filed it as live.  So this sweep separates
the two questions it conflated.

  the immutable revision  answers "is the published state correct?"
  the mutable path        answers "is that what a reader sees right now?"

Both were green at r131 for the first question.  This sweep is the second one, run after
the cache windows, in a real browser -- the session fetcher deduplicates within the hour, so
a re-read of a URL it has already seen returns the first answer and cannot settle anything.

fetched 2026-08-12, 12:50 JST (03:50 UTC), Chrome, no cache bypass, plain navigation.


1. https://mathlab0911.github.io/   -- the bare URL
-----------------------------------------------------
CAUGHT UP.  This is the surface that served the retracted page for about a minute at r131,
and the one printed in every author footnote and in the mail that went to two
mathematicians.  It now serves the rewrite:

  - opens with r_A(n) and the truncations, not with our own invariant;
  - the layer definition of Gamma, and 5.3492879... (not the banned 5.34920);
  - three parts, current titles, current statuses, including "MIXED BY SECTION" for III;
  - the sentence about one named open problem shared by three theorems;
  - a Use of AI tools section;
  - "Last updated: 12 August 2026".

No occurrence of the retired vocabulary, of "order-sensitive", or of four papers as a
present-tense claim.


2. https://github.com/mathlab0911/arithmetic-landscapes   -- the rendered README
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CAUGHT UP.  Rendered, not raw, because that is the page a reader lands on:

  - the opening paragraph defines r_A(n) and writes lm_A(n)/r_A(n) -> Gamma(A);
    deg_A does not appear anywhere on the page;
  - "every asymptotic statement in Parts II and III is unaffected" -- not "papers 2-4";
  - the papers table has exactly three rows, I / II / III;
  - the retirement paragraph is present, with the p3map pointer and "in the git history,
    not in the tree";
  - "nineteen mechanical checks";
  - the check table runs C1 to C19, with C19 reading "every Japanese edition has the same
    labels, theorem environments and status declarations as its source";
  - "127 scripts, 199 logs";
  - no BOM artefact at the head of the rendered document.


3. the About description  -- the class no check can reach
----------------------------------------------------------
CAUGHT UP.  It is not in any file, so the evidence is the page title, which GitHub builds
from it.  Served:

  "mathlab0911/arithmetic-landscapes: Arithmetic landscapes I-III: the gap series Gamma(A),
   and what it says about the local structure of subset sums.  Papers, a Lean 4
   formalisation with an independent kernel replay, and every numerical experiment with its
   log."

The pre-series title of Part I is gone.  fable's r132 asked for this specifically because
memory recorded the fix and a stale page was the only evidence against it -- which is the
same confusion the round is about, in the other direction: a stale surface is not evidence
that a change did not happen, any more than a fresh one is evidence that it did.  The
immutable check for a setting is that there is none; this is a human-verified item and the
ledger now says so.


verdict
-------
Three surfaces, three caught up, roughly twenty-two hours after the push.  Nothing
outstanding.  The r131 observation stands unchanged as a fact about the window and not
about the push: for some minutes after a push, the bare URL and the rendered README can
serve the previous state.


what this sweep could not see
-----------------------------
  - Any reader's own cache, which is not observable from here at all.
  - The PDFs as rendered by a viewer; only text surfaces were read.
  - Whether the mathematics is correct.  Nothing here is about that.
